---
title: Poached chicken and stock
description: Lesson 3.2. Gentle heat for meat, long heat for bones, and why stock sets to jelly.
servings: 4
time: 2 hours 30 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, wet-heat]
lesson: "3.2"
technique: poaching and stock
status: draft
---

> Technique: poaching, then stock. The meat is cooked gently and taken
> out as soon as it is done. The bones and skin go back in for much
> longer. The pot simmers the whole time and never boils.

Put @chicken thighs{1%kg}(bone-in, skin-on) in a #large pot{} with @onion{1}(halved), @carrot{1}(in chunks), @celery{1%stick}(in chunks), @bay leaf{1} and @black peppercorns{6}. Cover with @cold water{2%litres}.

> Why cold water: in a side-by-side test, stock started in cold water
> came out clearer than stock started in boiling water. Tasters found
> no difference in flavour.
-- source: "Hot or Cold Water for Stock?", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/6548-hot-or-cold-water-for-stock

Bring the pot slowly to a simmer over medium heat, with small bubbles breaking the surface now and then. Skim off the foam. Simmer for ~{30%minutes}, until the thickest part of a thigh, away from the bone, reads at least 74 °C on a #thermometer{}.

> Why not boil: the agitation of a boil breaks fat and soluble proteins
> into the liquid, and the stock turns cloudy.
-- source: "For a Clearer Stock, Simmer; Don't Boil", Cook's Illustrated, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/cooksillustrated/how_tos/9260-for-a-clearer-stock-simmer-dont-boil

> Safety: 74 °C is the safe minimum for poultry, read before the meat
> leaves the pot.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart", last updated 2025-04-14 (read from an archive copy), https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart : all poultry 165 °F (73.9 °C)

Lift the thighs out. When they are cool enough to handle, pull the meat off and keep it for the meal. Return the bones and skin to the pot.

Simmer the bones for ~{90%minutes} more. Strain the stock through a #sieve{} into a #wide bowl{}.

> Science: bones, skin and connective tissue are rich in collagen. In
> hot water collagen slowly unravels and dissolves as gelatin; in meat
> this starts above about 60 °C and only becomes significant near
> 80 °C, which is why it takes hours at a simmer. Gelatin forms a gel
> that is thermoreversible: it sets when cold and melts when warmed. A
> stock that turns to jelly in the fridge has plenty of it. Look up:
> collagen, gelatin, thermoreversible gel.
-- source: Ahmad et al., "Collagen and gelatin: Structure, properties, and applications in food industry", International Journal of Biological Macromolecules 254:128037, 2024, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijbiomac.2023.128037 ; Jiang et al., "Molecular mechanisms underlying sensory and chemical changes in muscle foods induced by sous-vide cooking: a review", Foods 14(17):2967, 2025, https://doi.org/10.3390/foods14172967 (collagen solubilisation "does not occur until 80 °C") ; "Braising 101", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/6353-braising-101 ; "Getting to Know: Braising", https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/10933-getting-to-know-braising (begins to break down beyond 140 degrees)

Cool the stock quickly: stand the bowl in cold water and stir now and then. Put it in the fridge as soon as it has stopped steaming.

> Safety: the official advice for cooked meat is to cool it as quickly
> as possible and then put it in the fridge or freezer; treat stock
> the same way. A deep pot of hot stock left on the hob cools
> slowly; a wide bowl in cold water cools fast.
-- source: Food Standards Scotland, "Food storage", https://www.foodstandards.gov.scot/consumers/food-safety/at-home/rice

> Next time, without the recipe: meat on the bone, cold water, a few
> aromatics, a simmer that never boils. Take the meat out when it
> reaches temperature and give the bones another hour or two.
